#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124265 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124461 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "b8884af1-186f-582b-8de2-a137a66649b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3432" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1,1)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+0+1)\\,i+(0+4y+0)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i+4y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2(1)+1)\\,i+4(1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\,i+4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124266 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/id #uuid "3ab5ece1-03aa-5fb0-9952-b858b6ca67b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3433" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124267 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "d3222782-abc1-5cd3-bceb-27e0585f9e09" :question/math-academy-id "e-3434" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2,$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$ can be found by computing \n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$   \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,\\sqrt{2}) & =(4\\cdot 2-3)\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5i-2j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i  - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124268 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/id #uuid "db58088e-59e0-5ca4-82a2-991bb6a4e7be" :question/math-academy-id "e-3435" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y-x})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{1-2})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0029eb5c-a868-5b35-b213-3fa6e3a04a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263005} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9853cb-1b4d-5d99-b740-f207d47a13c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263384} {:answer/id #uuid "62cb3b0d-c5e0-5140-9b14-0b73eecbc9ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263854} {:answer/id #uuid "70e32100-046e-5e0e-b9f2-eac7fe211b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263976} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d5900d-9aee-5b56-aa97-4e95d9c8f10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0029eb5c-a868-5b35-b213-3fa6e3a04a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263005} :answer-field/id #uuid "263638a6-9832-58e0-a5f7-da0d13d29e2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d42d4bf9-4eef-57b6-b4ed-d5448bfe2b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-130723" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $4xy+2{x}^{2}=x+2y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4xy+2{x}^{2} & =x+2y \\\\ 4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4y+4x-1)\\,i+(4x-2)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(4(-1)+4(2)-1)\\,i+(4(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨3,6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{6} \\\\ 6(x-2) & =3(y+1) \\\\ 2(x-2) & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-4 & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed1a1ab0-6d18-5f30-b80b-be9f46dd80b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398363} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cbc8d4-c2ee-5478-860e-c731a5274aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398364} {:answer/id #uuid "f499f957-93d0-5ca6-8e65-57c931e33b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398365} {:answer/id #uuid "d7aa6514-a3ad-578c-8808-8284836b7b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-2\\,j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398366} {:answer/id #uuid "431e58b2-3a4b-54f5-a2fc-05d378bf57ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f499f957-93d0-5ca6-8e65-57c931e33b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398365} :answer-field/id #uuid "530f90fa-b927-569c-b4dd-79831f2442af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da3be12b-b270-5258-9b1c-409842422d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-131116" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\text{ }i+2{z}^{2}y\\text{ }j+(2z{y}^{2}+2)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,-1) & =3(1)^{2}\\,i+2(-1)^{2}(-1)\\,j+(2(-1)(-1)^{2}+2)k \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-2\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed39b029-85c3-5e97-8954-e0c143d2660e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383225} {:answer/id #uuid "09fedb26-ce0e-5168-aa47-838277d2a8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383226} {:answer/id #uuid "f08ae5ee-40c5-51a6-9971-2dada660033f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383227} {:answer/id #uuid "0e4bca3e-62b0-506e-ab11-64664053f91f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383228} {:answer/id #uuid "ec84b4f7-88ba-52da-a5bb-02c8fc23e908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec84b4f7-88ba-52da-a5bb-02c8fc23e908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383229} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d2c4aa2-5988-5483-be94-986ea095a0e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14180128-1a3b-5df5-945e-e4a1d12a6375" :question/math-academy-id "q-131117" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}yz-y{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+({x}^{3}z-x{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+({x}^{3}y-2xyz)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,1) & =(3(1)^{2}(-2)(1)-(-2)(1)^{2})\\,i+((1)^{3}(1)-(1)(1)^{2})\\,j+((1)^{3}(-2)-2(1)(-2)(1))\\,k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-4\\,i+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026c9515-f47f-5873-a2ad-edb3df11eb6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,8⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383232} {:answer/id #uuid "701877fc-bf3b-56db-a45f-7c6a12a05e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383233} {:answer/id #uuid "b1dc8491-4f00-53bb-a2f3-1cc30657fc1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨4,-1,8⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383234} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb7af74-9d1c-58ad-9346-e5346aec63d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,8⟩+t\\,⟨-1,3,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383235} {:answer/id #uuid "bf422347-5e08-54dc-812b-c19e297a836d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,8⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "026c9515-f47f-5873-a2ad-edb3df11eb6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,8⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383232} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5bb21b4-51b2-5e7e-bdf4-252b406432c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fb8c052-74b8-5a3f-8748-9c302788bd84" :question/math-academy-id "q-131124" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}={x}^{2}-6x+12$ at the point $(2,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=12.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)k \\\\  & =(6-2x)\\,i-8y\\,j+8z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(2,0,1) & =(6-2(2))\\,i-8(0)\\text{ }j+8(1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+8\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(2,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(2,0,1)=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,8⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161e6fd0-e925-55fa-8d09-6a65d107f751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509728} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3d1a48-5c55-52da-b7e7-fd31db815995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-4,-4⟩+t\\,⟨0,2,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509817} {:answer/id #uuid "63801628-2a57-5777-861f-90cd5a83b242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-4,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509895} {:answer/id #uuid "77d647a0-cba4-5590-a1d5-ae95a15b5f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,-4,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509940} {:answer/id #uuid "af75210d-d1a7-57de-a250-0369260017a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-4,-4⟩+t\\,⟨2,2,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d647a0-cba4-5590-a1d5-ae95a15b5f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,-4,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509940} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885" :question/math-academy-id "q-131125" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=4-4z$ at the point $(0,2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =4-4z \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,2,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)k \\\\  & =2x\\,i-2y\\,j+4\\,k \\\\ ∇f(0,2,2) & =2(0)\\,i-2(2)\\text{ }j+4\\,k \\\\  & =-4\\,j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,2,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,2,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,2,2)=⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,-4,4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4345e16b-0df8-59b8-ad37-650db4ac210e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,3,-5⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263593} {:answer/id #uuid "58520017-6d75-5711-9c4c-279ff100c302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨-2,5,-3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263761} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8b5e1d-96ce-502e-ac9c-14b8cdee3daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264220} {:answer/id #uuid "a89fe459-90af-559f-b732-4d06764bcb9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264441} {:answer/id #uuid "e315f8f8-9238-56c6-b08f-3a589b1dff97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨4,5,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a89fe459-90af-559f-b732-4d06764bcb9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264441} :answer-field/id #uuid "c90461b1-1149-54d3-9d43-9942adac1495" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b9d3e0c-64e0-51a4-97be-e5302f43c4c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-131126" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x=5$ at the point $(1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=5.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)k \\\\  & =(2xy+{z}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+2yz)\\,j+({y}^{2}+2zx)\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(2(1)(-1)+(2)^{2})\\text{ }i+((1)^{2}+2(-1)(2))\\text{ }j+((-1)^{2}+2(2)(1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,-1,2)$ and passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,∇f(1,-1,2)=⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124461 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a93dcc0e-bacf-58c5-b6f4-47f43da168f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122415} {:answer/id #uuid "370200a2-bd3f-5447-b9b1-ea23d53237b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122417} {:answer/id #uuid "42dfcda5-2b9e-563b-b4e1-fb6d44bf17e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122418} {:answer/id #uuid "1c93b689-6924-505a-914c-f48d03994cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509745} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c75248-f110-5d02-9855-a77023469031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "370200a2-bd3f-5447-b9b1-ea23d53237b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122417} :answer-field/id #uuid "37f65865-99f1-54aa-953e-7c631fab08cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e917aee-92ff-5d58-8526-d5d5bb6b5240" :question/math-academy-id "q-36311" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4x+0-0)\\,i+(0+4y-1)\\,j \\\\  & =4x\\,i+(4y-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =4(0)\\,i+(4(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∇f(0,1)}{‖∇f(0,1)‖}=\\frac{3\\,j}{3}=j\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c70724-aaae-5f8b-90ba-0c075fe647ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321579} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3836ff-c967-550a-b987-849d6e75299d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321892} {:answer/id #uuid "62b7f57c-9049-5269-a2d4-b7a2f5cbdbc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321917} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6b18d1-1602-5fab-90d5-83a3fb2ed5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322404} {:answer/id #uuid "cdecf84e-1100-5475-ac8c-0fad8bf69a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62b7f57c-9049-5269-a2d4-b7a2f5cbdbc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321917} :answer-field/id #uuid "8225f4bf-8597-5194-8000-7f67e378df28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aec682f0-94b9-5c3a-a680-f47e16fecc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-36339" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $6xy=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(10x-6y)\\,i+(2y-6x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(10-6(1))\\,i+(2(1)-6)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,1)=⟨4,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{4} & =\\frac{y-1}{-4} \\\\ x-1 & =-y+1 \\\\ x+y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124461 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144e5910-b093-5cd4-abe0-394d07cded53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,i-12\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509724} {:answer/id #uuid "3c60959a-01b7-53b2-91be-83d1b906ea40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,i+7\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509819} {:answer/id #uuid "812ac18a-9cbd-56eb-aadd-016577600a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509971} {:answer/id #uuid "91e9dfc1-cddb-5022-b3ab-97f0a0267baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509997} {:answer/id #uuid "cef8e14b-ece7-51a1-921e-23d70f9d2c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cef8e14b-ece7-51a1-921e-23d70f9d2c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510126} :answer-field/id #uuid "b33a5905-3216-54dd-ad86-072ac416bdd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e2999c4-1892-5dd4-a533-5ed9b93e52c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-36378" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2x+4y+5xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+4y+5xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+4y+5xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(5y+2)\\text{ }i+(5x+4)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(5\\cdot 1+2)\\text{ }i+(5\\cdot 2+4)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =7i+14j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,1)}{‖∇f(2,1)‖} & =\\frac{7i+14j}{\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(14)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7i+14j}{7\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aae3ac3-02f2-5d2e-bd85-9eee8e2b917d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=t,\\text{ }y=t,\\text{ }z=\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509950} {:answer/id #uuid "84d505d2-2d10-57ad-9f74-f1753c66adec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-t,\\text{ }y=0,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{t\\sqrt{e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509979} {:answer/id #uuid "ad368c2c-26d6-548c-b643-eedd79a18a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=t,\\text{ }y=0,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{t\\sqrt{e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510059} {:answer/id #uuid "f1711e24-9959-5e3d-beef-58d26c1f41b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\text{ }y=0,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510207} {:answer/id #uuid "f43507f5-6454-586e-8980-28bd1f31c21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\text{ }y=t,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+2t\\sqrt{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1711e24-9959-5e3d-beef-58d26c1f41b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\text{ }y=0,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510207} :answer-field/id #uuid "c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-37231" :question/problem "Find parametric equations for the normal line to the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=1$ at the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e}).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}).$ Then,  our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e})$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,\\sqrt{e}).$  \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,\\sqrt{e}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,\\sqrt{e}) & =\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{e}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe parametric equations for the normal line to $f(x,y,z)$ at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ are given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={x}_{0}+{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t, \\\\ y & ={y}_{0}+{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t, \\\\ z & ={z}_{0}+{f}_{z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametic equations  for the normal line to our surface that  passes through the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e})$ can be written as \n \n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=0,\\,z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a64d29b1-e2ba-5e48-b58f-cbe663d22375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{2-z}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385379} {:answer/id #uuid "12178fb2-90d2-55f5-8547-92f9528d658e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385380} {:answer/id #uuid "81c5f623-6c5c-5ccc-9c48-e4f0e0bb30c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{z-2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385381} {:answer/id #uuid "80aa2bbc-5891-5ead-81a6-a88d99d82419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385382} {:answer/id #uuid "c7afb82c-1d04-5f3a-b6b5-2604eb2833f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=y-1=\\frac{2-z}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12178fb2-90d2-55f5-8547-92f9528d658e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385380} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b8dbb5c-29ef-5a51-b4a1-be1ea897551a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "780bb1b8-cd5e-553c-8c69-1da3293bbe1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37232" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}=1$ at the point $(2,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$     \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1,2).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1,2):$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }j-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,2) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }j-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by using the symmetric form of the equation of the line that is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$,  we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{\\frac{1}{2}}=\\frac{z-2}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a68112ca-e7ee-5f12-8c6b-6f964b2fce64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,i+4j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398915} {:answer/id #uuid "5739b0cd-fba7-5818-952f-616d15f8cd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i-4j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398916} {:answer/id #uuid "fc61dd7b-3fa9-5347-ad13-eae563990528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398917} {:answer/id #uuid "9e270cd2-2ed5-5ff9-a8d2-046b4683fe91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,i+4j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398918} {:answer/id #uuid "12f59294-d897-5332-9fd4-6a0a58840be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-2j+6\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5739b0cd-fba7-5818-952f-616d15f8cd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i-4j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398916} :answer-field/id #uuid "027e0dd0-8fb0-53bb-95b9-27c1e0e20a6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15fe86f3-62bf-5e0d-8567-00ad11df9f8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-65167" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-2yz+z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,k \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\text{ }i+(2xy-2z)\\text{ }j+(-2y+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+(2(1)(-1)-2(1))\\,j+(-2(-1)+1)\\,k \\\\  & =1\\,i-4j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\,i-4\\,j+3\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3784325a-a369-50b5-91bd-19b0506f0065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333499} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f25158-cf49-5e7b-a678-2a7edff63f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334412} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b97395-2591-5fe7-a765-24e75cd37f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334425} {:answer/id #uuid "e42e7344-8685-5612-9437-27f80a5f22bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334977} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b5911-a43a-5716-a002-b2a98cb3a797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i-\\,j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3784325a-a369-50b5-91bd-19b0506f0065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333499} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d7321b-1e15-5d1e-9dec-8cc8fec04036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f338c4bb-049a-5229-858d-81a11a4613ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-65168" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,1,-1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,k \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+2xz)\\text{ }i+(2xy+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+(2yz+{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,1,-1) & =((1)^{2}+2(-1)(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)(1)+(-1)^{2}))\\,j+(2(1)(-1)+(-1)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i-1j-1\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124461 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157bc3da-2d1c-5137-b516-d8ff7c42bad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336235} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfe7ebf-3262-597f-b2fd-edf534fd618c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336607} {:answer/id #uuid "4baa559e-cde4-5072-9145-09ac20cb641a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336735} {:answer/id #uuid "829583d7-8e78-579d-9221-64ce8b48dc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337220} {:answer/id #uuid "e26af925-4997-53e5-8b3d-7425b92d8e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4baa559e-cde4-5072-9145-09ac20cb641a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336735} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48649d2-bf76-55fc-aef9-b0aff8e82c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22cfc584-e8e6-55e0-a1d3-35d71c5742a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-71370" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, can be found by computing $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =2(-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{ }i+2(\\frac{1}{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}{‖∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})‖} & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124461 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049b7f23-d6a7-5be3-a6ca-ac19b8a84234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336070} {:answer/id #uuid "123c36c6-7310-581c-8de4-b675e090dc56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336203} {:answer/id #uuid "4b49d759-d015-5843-b6e9-3497dc09edfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336730} {:answer/id #uuid "6e16ffd6-406d-50b5-9b6a-0f4b899fc6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337009} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd3f738-ef19-5d09-afcb-04a5b6bdd7fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(-2i+i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e16ffd6-406d-50b5-9b6a-0f4b899fc6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337009} :answer-field/id #uuid "60184a90-8959-560b-aa4a-378830d13dbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "436e814d-020d-5b28-b909-495bbb1c431a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71374" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x+3y-2xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(1-2y)\\text{ }i+(3-2x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1) & =(1-2(1))\\text{ }i+(3-2(\\frac{3}{2}))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1)}{‖∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1)‖} & =\\frac{-i}{1}=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124461 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2372ab35-9f15-566a-9150-808733fd4bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336363} {:answer/id #uuid "2630b03f-d66e-5733-b918-78baaf9f7ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336392} {:answer/id #uuid "e77b993b-c3bb-5f5a-9474-9bd52741e5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338055} {:answer/id #uuid "e9097253-bc27-5ce4-8fb8-daded67d09c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338066} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a289db-1c93-5c45-ab8f-caa51a0ee7e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6a289db-1c93-5c45-ab8f-caa51a0ee7e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338195} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d94b131-873d-56a9-92e8-a2ed25af773a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1092dcda-9470-51be-bb7f-4be55bcc6069" :question/math-academy-id "q-71381" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}+x+y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,0) & =({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(0,0)}{‖∇f(0,0)‖} & =\\frac{2i+2j}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2i+2j}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65c88cb4-d780-590b-b992-1f5a2d099b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336940} {:answer/id #uuid "b17a59bd-34cb-524b-a586-d668f0ab265c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337621} {:answer/id #uuid "b93bd652-ee1e-5354-8880-3331e0abdb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337674} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e5d67c-091f-5e66-b35b-d7bf5ddf3a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337771} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd19af6-18df-5dbc-8ca0-9ef0b733476e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcd19af6-18df-5dbc-8ca0-9ef0b733476e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338255} :answer-field/id #uuid "947eebdf-2bd7-5433-b16d-dbcb38a79114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "05c970aa-8c53-5d2c-be67-cb9e3da21e11" :question/math-academy-id "q-71385" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =2(2)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨4,0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)0-(y-0)4 & =0 \\\\ -4y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241c7529-7f80-5365-a0c6-f7e836aaee88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+x+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336372} {:answer/id #uuid "391f7649-2e76-52fa-81b6-233716b6923c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+3x+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336569} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb41a0a-f9a7-5281-9885-444e698af19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-x+8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336998} {:answer/id #uuid "b3143694-8d1c-519f-bf94-4aa69b2c3b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+2x+11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337629} {:answer/id #uuid "f204000c-6f32-5823-8c04-611b3011d48d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+3x+20=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "241c7529-7f80-5365-a0c6-f7e836aaee88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+x+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336372} :answer-field/id #uuid "4287e815-efb6-5dc2-9403-e74ef29f8446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1df9544-1831-5471-b207-bb07d395022e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71390" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}=4xy.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =4xy \\\\ {y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(6x-4y)\\,i+(2y-4x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,-3) & =(6(-1)-4(-3))\\,i+(2(-3)-4(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-2\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,-3)=⟨6,-2⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{6} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-2} \\\\ (-2)(x+1) & =6(y+3) \\\\ -2x-2 & =6y+18 \\\\ 6y+2x+20 & =0 \\\\ 3y+x+10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "923db4e3-1672-5a50-9581-8d103a4e9394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337357} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ca54cd-1662-5f06-ad4a-139620f93cc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337484} {:answer/id #uuid "a201c603-2105-5c16-ac28-6ea1909ee0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337500} {:answer/id #uuid "f0aa3752-46b2-55e2-872a-d4d7eb4de111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338139} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b5b220-5b4e-5b43-8acc-b11f2fdd7f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a201c603-2105-5c16-ac28-6ea1909ee0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337500} :answer-field/id #uuid "a093c235-9586-5c2c-a6e1-6f61aa48334d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47afea57-2ac6-5bfd-8472-520b5199e74f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71393" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)=2xy.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & =2xy \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2y)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(0))\\,j \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,1)=⟨-1,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1} & =\\frac{y-1}{1} \\\\ -x & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4643060a-8d1d-5789-a362-102d70382db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398956} {:answer/id #uuid "8070bb66-d16c-505c-85ee-2a417bc81d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398957} {:answer/id #uuid "0bdcaf70-4358-5973-a594-026557c83288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398958} {:answer/id #uuid "61e68fe8-0332-53d0-b214-d38a39b52899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398959} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c9c882-083f-5978-8947-be71bba2f10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4643060a-8d1d-5789-a362-102d70382db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398956} :answer-field/id #uuid "91095769-6695-5735-bdf7-d8d4bfdca38b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58a67c4a-aa8b-5b64-8268-56928e2716de" :question/math-academy-id "q-71399" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(0,0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,0) & =({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,i+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,j+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$"}]}
